## Claim
Text: "A third of excess deaths in the United States between 1 March and  1 August 2020 during the COVID-19 pandemic could not be directly attributed to the coronavirus"
Claim author: Michael Lee  Washington Examiner
Claim date: October 18, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210310055253/https://archive.is/EKPAJ

## Initial Q&A
### What was the total number of excess deaths in the United States between March 1 and August 1, 2020, according to the data source used in the claim?
Answer: According to the search result, there were 1,336,561 total deaths in the United States between March 1 and August 1, 2020, with 225,530 of those being excess deaths.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

### How many of those excess deaths were officially recorded as directly caused by COVID-19 (i.e., with COVID-19 listed as the underlying cause of death) during that period?
Answer: According to the study cited in the Fox News article, of the 225,530 excess deaths in the U.S. between March and August 2020, 150,541 were attributed to COVID-19 (i.e., listed as the underlying cause of death).

Source URL: https://www.foxnews.com/health/coronavirus-us-excess-deaths-study

### What specific definition of "excess deaths" was employed in the claim, including the baseline expected number of deaths and the statistical method used to calculate it?
Answer: The claim in the Record references a JAMA study that defined "excess deaths" as the number of deaths above the expected baseline, which was calculated as the average number of deaths from the previous five years for the same time period. Specifically, the study found 87,001 excess deaths from March 1 to April 25, 2020, using this five-year average as the expected count. The statistical method was a straightforward comparison of observed deaths to the historical average, without more complex modeling.

Source URL: https://news.vcu.edu/article/jama_study_35_of_excess_deaths_during_pandemics_early_months

### Which organization or dataset (e.g., CDC's National Center for Health Statistics, state health departments) provided the mortality data that underlies the claim?
Answer: The mortality data underlying the claim came from the **National Center for Health Statistics (NCHS)** and the **U.S. Census Bureau**, as analyzed by researchers from Virginia Commonwealth University and Yale in the study published in *JAMA*.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

### What criteria were used to classify a death as "directly attributed" to the coronavirus, and did this classification include only laboratory-confirmed cases or also probable cases?
Answer: The criteria used to classify a death as "directly attributed" to COVID-19, according to the CDC's National Center for Health Statistics, is that the death certificate lists COVID-19 as a cause of death, with the death coded to ICD-10 code U07.1. This classification includes both confirmed and presumed or probable cases, "with or without laboratory confirmation." Specifically, the source states: "When COVID-19 is reported as a cause of death – or when it is listed as a 'probable' or 'presumed' cause — the death is coded as U07.1. This can include cases with or without laboratory confirmation."

Source URL: https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nvss/vsrr/covid19/index.htm

### Did the claim account for deaths that were indirectly caused by the pandemic (e.g., from delayed medical care, drug overdoses, or mental health crises) when stating that one-third were not directly attributed to COVID-19?
Answer: Yes, the claim accounted for deaths indirectly caused by the pandemic. The study cited in the search result explicitly states that the 33% of excess deaths not directly attributed to COVID-19 include "people who did not have Covid-19, but died because of disruptions caused by the pandemic," such as "someone who has chest pain, who is scared to call 911" and dies of a heart attack, as well as increased mortality from "substance abuse and decreases in access to medical care." Thus, the claim's "one-third" figure encompasses indirect pandemic-related deaths.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/covid-19-pandemic-has-claimed-far-more-lives-reported-study-n1242970

### How does the claim's figure of one-third of excess deaths not directly attributed to COVID-19 compare with other independent analyses of excess mortality in the United States for the same time period?
Answer: The independent analysis published in JAMA and reported by Fox News found that 67% of excess deaths during March–July 2020 were attributed to COVID-19, meaning that 33% (approximately one-third) were not directly attributed to the coronavirus. This directly corroborates the claim's figure of one-third of excess deaths not directly attributed to COVID-19.

Source URL: https://www.foxnews.com/health/coronavirus-us-excess-deaths-study

### Was the claim published on October 18, 2020, based on data that were available at that time, and have subsequent revisions or updates to the mortality data changed the proportion of excess deaths not directly attributed to COVID-19?
Answer: The search result confirms that the claim published on October 18, 2020, was based on data available at that time, as a study published in JAMA on October 12, 2020, found that from March through July 2020, "deaths directly linked to Covid-19 account for 67 percent of those excess deaths, leaving the remaining 33 percent without a clear explanation," which directly matches the claim's assertion that a third of excess deaths were not directly attributed to the coronavirus. However, the search result does not provide any subsequent revisions or updates to mortality data that would indicate a change in this proportion after that date.

Source URL: https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/covid-19-pandemic-has-claimed-far-more-lives-reported-study-n1242970

### Did the author, Michael Lee, or the Washington Examiner provide a specific citation, study, or methodology in the original article that supports the claim that a third of excess deaths were not directly attributable to the coronavirus?
Answer: Yes, the original article by Michael Lee (or the Washington Examiner) likely cited the same JAMA study referenced in the CNN report, which found that COVID-19 was a documented cause of only 67% of excess deaths, meaning roughly one-third were not directly attributable to the coronavirus. The CNN article explicitly states: “Covid-19 was a documented cause of only 67% of these excess deaths,” and attributes the data to a study by Dr. Steven Woolf and colleagues published in JAMA, which used death data from the National Center for Health Statistics and the US Census Bureau.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

### What was the geographic scope of the data (e.g., all 50 states and D.C.) and were any adjustments made for reporting delays or incomplete death certificates in the calculation of excess deaths?
Answer: Based on the search result, the geographic scope of the data was the entire United States, covering the period from March 1 to August 1, 2020. The study acknowledged limitations including the use of "provisional data" and "inaccuracies in death certificates," but the provided excerpt does not specifically mention any adjustments made for reporting delays or incomplete death certificates in the calculation of excess deaths.

Source URL: https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html

## Final Judgement
The fact-check confirms that a JAMA study published on October 12, 2020, found that out of 225,530 excess deaths in the U.S. between March 1 and August 1, 2020, 150,541 (67%) were directly attributed to COVID-19, leaving approximately 33% (one-third) not directly attributed to the coronavirus. This figure includes deaths indirectly caused by the pandemic, such as those from delayed medical care or drug overdoses. The claim aligns precisely with this study, which was based on data from the National Center for Health Statistics and the U.S. Census Bureau. No evidence contradicts the claim or suggests alternative interpretations.

