### Verdict: SUPPORTED

### Justification
A JAMA study published on October 12, 2020, analyzed 225,530 excess deaths in the U.S. between March 1 and August 1, 2020, using data from the [National Center for Health Statistics and the U.S. Census Bureau](https://www.cnn.com/2020/10/12/health/us-deaths-covid-19-pandemic-jama-study/index.html). It found that 150,541 (67%) were directly attributed to COVID-19, leaving approximately 33% (one-third) not directly attributed to the coronavirus. This remainder includes deaths indirectly caused by the pandemic, such as from delayed medical care or drug overdoses, as noted in [NBC News](https://www.nbcnews.com/health/health-news/covid-19-pandemic-has-claimed-far-more-lives-reported-study-n1242970). The claim aligns precisely with this study, and no evidence contradicts it.